{
  "version": "1.0",
  "name": "Quantum 2: Entangled Neurons",
  "description": "Quantum Entanglement in Neural Processing\n\nWHAT IS ENTANGLEMENT?\nWhen quantum systems become entangled, measuring one instantly affects \nthe other - regardless of distance. Einstein called this \"spooky action \nat a distance.\"\n\nIN THE BRAIN:\nPenrose-Hameroff propose that microtubules across different neurons could \nbecome quantum entangled, allowing instantaneous coordination - potentially \nexplaining the \"binding problem\" (how the brain unifies experience).\n\nTHIS DEMONSTRATION:\nTwo quantum microtubules process DIFFERENT inputs but share entanglement \nthrough the entanglement_range parameter. Watch how their outputs become \ncorrelated even with different stimuli.\n\nTHE BINDING PROBLEM:\nHow does your brain combine:\n  - Color from visual cortex\n  - Shape from another region  \n  - Sound from auditory cortex\n  - Memory associations\n  \n...into ONE unified experience of seeing a red apple and hearing a crunch?\n\nClassical neuroscience struggles to explain this timing problem.\nQuantum entanglement could allow instantaneous binding across brain regions.\n\nEXPERIMENT:\n- Vary the two inputs independently\n- Observe correlations in the outputs (Correlation node)\n- Increase entanglement_range to strengthen the effect\n- Compare to classical LSTM nodes (no spooky correlation!)",
